The drive_server program
---------------------------------

The drive_server program must be running a machine somewhere on your
network in order for DRIVE to work.  The drive_server program may be
started manually in the following manner:


   $ drive_server &

(NOTE:  the '&' at the end of the command line runs the program as a
background task.)

The server program expects no options.  Once the server is running, 
the client programs may connect from other workstations:

   $ drive <hostname>

where <hostname> is the name of the machine running the drive_server
program.

This program is responsible for keeping track of the "world" of the
Driving Simulator.  It updates all the drive client programs
(described below) based on the user's input, the physics of the
simulated world, the interaction of a driver's vehicle with the
environment and other vehicles, and so on.

If a drive program is started and there is no drive_server running
on the local network, the user can choose to start a drive_server on 
his local system 

The DRIVE Program
====================

When started, the client program, "drive", begins by creating two X11
windows on your display.  One window is used to select the vehicle you
wish to drive, the other is the window that will be used to do the
driving.  (Note that the behavior described here is the "default"
behavior.  Many aspects of the drive program can be modified by a
.driverc file, described later).

Selecting a Vehicle
-------------------
The smaller of the two initial windows is entitled "Vehicle Selection_popup".  
This window consists of three parts:
 1) a list of vehicles available
 2) Three Slider Bars and a "Color" indicator
 3) an "OK" button

    1) a list of vehicles available
       This list shows all the vehicles known to the server program.  To view
       any of these vehicles, simply click the name of the vehicle with the
       left mouse button.  This "selects" the vehicle for viewing -- the 
       vehicle is displayed in the large "drive" window, rotating.

    2) Three Slider Bars and a "Color" indicator
       Underneath the list of vehicles is a label 'Color: <colorname>',
       and three slider bars.  The <colorname> reflects the state of the
       three sliders, which can be used to choose any of a large number 
       of colors.  The sliders work in an 'HSV' model, or Hue, Saturation,
       and Value.  The top slider controls the 'Hue' of the vehicle, i.e.,
       Red, Yellow, Blue, Green, etc.

       The second slider controls the 'Saturation' of the color: the further
       left the slider is, the more 'washed out' the color is (tending 
       toward White, or grey).  If you are unfamiliar with the HSV model,
       you could probably move this slider all the way to the right.

       The third slider controls the 'Value' of the color; how light or
       dark it is.  The further right the slider is, the brighter the color.

    3) an "OK" button
       Once you have selected the vehicle you want, and have chosen a color,
       you may begin driving the vehicle by clicking this button. NOTE: When
       this button is clicked, the "Vehicle Selection_popup" window disappears.


Driving a Vehicle
----------------------
Once you have selected a vehicle and joined the game (described above), you
can now "drive" your vehicle in the Three Dimensional "world" scene. The
"Driving Simulator" window has several parts:
 1) An upper viewing area window, where the world scene may be viewed.
 2) A smaller viewing area window, showing the vehicle's dashboard, 
    instruments, gauges, gears, and so forth.
 3) A Text window, where various messages can be displayed.
 4) A row of buttons.


   1) Upper Viewing area window 
      This is the window that you will use to control your vehicle, and to
      see what is in the world scene around your vehicle.  The controls for
      your vehicle can be seen by clicking the "Help" button in
      the row of buttons at the bottom of the "Driving Simulator" window,
      and selecting the "Controls" menu item.

   2) Lower Viewing area window 
      This window contains a picture of the vehicle's dashboard, as well as 
      a Speedometer, a Tachometer, and a gear selection indicator.  (The
      gear indicator in red is the currently selected gear).  Also, the 
      position of the stick shift indicates which gear the vehicle is in.

   3) Row of buttons
      The following buttons are found at the bottom of the "Driving Simulator"
      window:

      Vehicle -- Allows you to select Automatic (default) or Manual 
		 Transmission.
      
      Windows -- Allows you to select a Large or Small driving window,
		 turn on or off Radar and Race Standings windows, constrain
		 the cursor, and select more or less detailed graphics 
		 models.

      Upright -- Will upright your vehicle if you end up on your side or top.

      Start Over -- Will place your vehicle back at the starting point.

      Quit -- Terminates the drive program.

      Help -- Brings up a menu of options; selecting one of the options will
	      bring up a window with help (text or graphics ) for the selected
	      topic.


Driving Modes
-----------------------

Regular Viewing Mode:
  This is the mode that the driving simulator begins in.  The view is as if
  from the driver's seat of the vehicle, looking out the front windshield.
  There are keys that allow you to look out the side and back "windows" of
  the vehicle (the "Controls" menu option of the "Help" button, contains 
  this information).

Skycam Mode:
  When is Skycam Mode, the view seen is as though from a point outside your
  vehicle, always looking at the center of your vehicle.  The position from
  which you view your vehicle can be adjusted by certain keys  (arrow keys
  to adjust the angle from which you view your vehicle, Prev and Next keys
  to zoom out or in.  By moving the viewing position far away from your 
  vehicle, you may also see much of the other objects in the world scene.

There are a few keys that are not documented in the "Controls" 
pop up window.  Some of these are noted here:

1) Pressing the Space Bar will apply full brakes to your vehicle.
   The mouse remains where you have positioned it, but brakes are applied 
   for as long as the Space Bar is pressed.  Releasing the Space Bar will
   allow the acceleration to return to normal.

2) Pressing and holding the <shift> key prior to using the <Prev> and <Next>
   keys in Skycam mode will increase the Zoom in/out speed.

3) Pressing the 't' key gives your vehicle a Turbo boost -- use this
   carefully!  Turbo boosts are generated while the 't' key is pressed.
   (This also supplies Turbo brakes).  Releasing the 't' key shuts off
   turbo boosts.  Note that using Turbo can sometimes cause localized
   temporal distortion fields which tear the fabric of the space-time
   continuum, resulting in the suspension of certain laws of simulator
   physics.  Don't be too surpized by visual and physical abnormalities
   if you spend much time in this mode.


Example .driverc file
----------------------------

# A .driverc created in your $HOME directory can be used to customize your
# DRIVE environment.  Lines beginning with '#' and blank lines are ignored.
# The following options are recognized:
#
#  Vehicle: {Sports Car, Sedan, Minivan, Tank}
#     Use the specified vehicle.
#
#  Color:  <r> <g> <b>
#     Specify rgb color where <r>,<g>, and <b> are between 0.0 and 1.0.
#
#  Autostart
#     Use the specified vehicle and color (defaults if not specified) and
#     do not bring up the Vehicle Selection Window.
#
#  Server: <server machine>
#     Specify the name of the machine where the drive_server program is running.
#     
#  Confine Cursor
#     Start the simulation with the cursor confined to the window.
#
#  Confine Small
#     Start the simulation with the cursor confined to the driving window.
#     In this configuration, the cursor cannot be moved to the row of 
#     buttons at the bottom of the screen (i.e. Quit, Upright, ... )
#     This mode is good for races and/or when using a steering wheel
#     with the mouse.
#
#  Overhead Camera
#     This option will start up the program in "skycam" mode instead of 
#     in the driver's seat.
#
#  Camera Up Angle
#     Controls the height of the camera in "Overhead Camera" mode.
#
#  Camera Around Angle
#     Controls the position of the camera in a circle around the car
#     in "Overhead Camera" mode.
#
#  Camera Distance
#     Controls the distance from the car in "Overhead Camera" mode.
#
#  Automatic Camera
#     A view that slowly rotates around the car being driven.  This is
#     useful when combined with the "Automatic Watch Mode" option.
#
#  Watch Mode
#     Allows the user to connect to the server as a spectator instead
#     of a driver.  No car will be created for this connection, but 
#     the user will be allowed to see the actions of other drivers.
#     To switch to watching the next player, use the "Restart" button.
#
#  Automatic Watch Mode
#     This is the same as "Watch Mode" except that the program switches
#     automatically from connection to connection.
#
#  Automatic Watch Frames
#     This option controls how quickly a program in "Automatic Watch Mode"
#     will switch between connections.
#
#  Small Window
#     Starts the simulation using a smaller window.  This allows
#     increased driving frame rates.
#  
#  Scene X: <x>
#  Scene Z: <z>
#     Specifies the coordinates of the Scene you wish to be started 
#     (and re-started) in.
#     The default scene is Noobyville (scene location 0, -2).  These 
#     numbers must be integers!
#
#  Position: <x> <y> <z>
#      Specifies the x, y, and z offset (in feet) from the origin of the
#      scene where you start (and re-start).  The default places you in the
#      parkinglot in Noobyville.  This position can also be specified by
#      the following three values:
#  X: <x>
#  Y: <y>
#  Z: <z>
#
#  Angle: <angle>
#      Specifies the direction your car faces when starting (or re-starting).
#      This number represents degrees, with 0 facing due North, 90 facing
#      West, 180 facing South, and 270 facing due East.  The default
#      is 0 (North).
#
# Example:
#Vehicle: Sports Car
#Color: 0.1 0.2 0.2
#Autostart
#Server: driver
#Confine Cursor
#Confine Small
#Overhead Camera
#Camera Up Angle: 40
#Camera Around Angle: 90
#Camera Distance: 100
#Automatic Camera
#Watch Mode
#Automatic Watch Mode
#Automatic Watch Frames: 5
#Small Window
# The next 4 lines position a car in the main intersection in Noobyville,
# facing East.
#Scene X: -2
#Scene Z: 0
#Position: 0.0 0.0 0.0
#Angle: -90.0


Example .server_config file
----------------------------

# The .server_config file in  <TBD> can be used to customize 
# your DRIVE server environment.  Lines beginning with '#' and blank lines 
# are ignored.
# The following options are recognized:
#
#  Normal Mode
#      The default drive server mode.  
#
#  Demo Mode
#      Brings up the server interface for running races on any of the race
#      tracks.  The initial mode (for client programs) is a welcome screen 
#      that shows drivers the color of their cars.  Next is a practice mode 
#      followed by the actual race.  The transition to each of these modes is 
#      controlled by a button on the server interface.  
#
#  Practice Seconds
#      Sets the duration of the practice in the "Demo" modes.
#
#  Race Seconds
#      Sets the duration of the race in the "Demo" modes.
#
#  Start SceneX:  <x_scene>
#      Defines the X coordinate of the scene that vehicles will start in
#      (by default).  A user may override this default (and the default for
#      Start SceneZ:, Start Position:, and Start Angle:) by setting values
#      in the .driverc file (see above), unless Demo Mode is turned on in
#      this file.  If Demo Mode is on, the vehicles are forced to start as 
#      specified here.
#
#  Start SceneZ: <z_scene>
#      Defines the Z coordinate of the scene that vehicles will start in
#      (by default).
#
#
#  Start Position: <x> <y> <z>
#      Defines the x,y,z position within the starting scene for the first 
#      vehicle to be placed. (Subsequent vehicles are placed from that point,
#      offset by constant amounts depending on when they enter).
#
#  Start Angle: <angle>
#      Defines the direction that the vehicles will face (by default) when
#      then enter (and restart).  0 is North, 90 is West, 180 is South,
#      and 270 is East.
#
#  Start Positions: <number>
#      Defines the number of horizontal positions for vehicles entering the
#      game.  If more that this number of vehicles enter the game, they will
#      be given starting positions in "rows" behind the other cars.
#
#  Turbo Mode: <value>
#      Either allows or prohibits users from using the "turbo" key.  To allow
#      unlimited turbo boosts, the value should be equal to 1. 
#      To prohibit turbo, value should be equal to 0.  Setting the value to 2
#      allows limited turbo boosts (See "Turbo Boosts:" below)
#
#  Turbo Boosts: <number>
#       Specifies the number of turbo boosts each vehicle may use for a 
#       "race".  (The number used by the vehicle may be reset to 0 each time
#       a "START" type checkpoint object is passed.)  Note that holding 
#       down the 't' key may generate MANY turbo boosts.
#
#  Guns Mode:
#      Either allows or prohibits users from using Guns.  To allow
#      unlimited guns, the value should be equal to 1. 
#      To prohibit guns, value should be equal to 0.  Setting the value to 2
#      allows limited guns boosts (See "Ammo:" below)
#
#  Ammo:
#      Specifies the number of shells or mines each vehicle may use for a 
#      "race".  (The number used by the vehicle may be reset to 0 each time
#      a "START" type checkpoint object is passed.)  
#      The effect of a shell (or mine) exploding near a vehicle can be set 
#      with the next 4 entries:
#
#  Explosions Disabled
#      No explosions allowed, and hence, no Guns or mines allowed.  Has the
#      Same effect as setting Guns Mode to 0.
#  Explosions Visual
#      Explosions are seen, but have no effect on vehicles.
#  Explosions Force
#      Explosions cause a random force to be applied to affected vehicles.
#  Explosions Restart
#      Explosions cause the vehicle to 'Restart'. (Does not currently work).
#
#  Checkpoint Reload
#       This option allows Checkpoints (including Start objects) to 'reload'
#       Turbo Boosts, Ammo, or both.  The number of turbos and amount of
#       ammo reloaded by each checkpoint is specified individually for each
#       Checkpoint in the scene description. 
#
#  Spacecraft Races
#       Enables checkpoint registering for Space Vehicles (allowing them
#       to participate in races).
#
#  Allow Spacecraft
#       Allows Spacecraft type vehicles to be driven.  By default, these 
#       vehicles are not allowed.
#
#  Disallow Tanks
#       Prevents Tank type vehicles from being driven.  By default, these 
#       vehicles are allowed.
#
#  Disallow Motorcycles
#       Prevents Motorcycle type vehicles from being driven.  By default, 
#       these vehicles are allowed.
#
#  Disallow UFOs
#       Prevents UFO type vehicles from being driven.  By default, these 
#       vehicles are allowed.
#
#  Reread Scenefiles
#       Causes the drive_server program to continually scan the scenes/
#       subdirectory to see if any of the scene files have been modified.
#       If a file has been modified, the drive_server will re-read that
#       file, throwing away, then re-generating the scenes described in
#       that file.  This is useful for interactively designing race courses
#       or otherwise laying out scenes.
#
###Examples:
#
#Normal Mode
#Demo Mode
#Practice Seconds: 120
#Race Seconds: 120

# The following settings could be used to start races in Grrville....

#Start SceneX: 4
#Start SceneZ: -1 
#Start Position: -23 1 -100
#Start Angle: 0
#Start Positions: 4
#Turbo Mode: 2
#Turbo Boosts: 10
